// bouncing — a program, not a package.
//
// Nothing imports this. It is the *device glue* for one platform: the manifest, the Gradle build,
// the activity SDL needs on the Java side, and the CMake that links what sysl compiles into the
// `.so` Android loads. `picokit` says the same thing about a carrier board — "This is a program, not
// a package. Nothing imports it" — and this is that arrangement moved to a phone.
//
// **The reason it cannot be a package is `classes.jar`.** SDL's Android support is half C and half
// Java, and the Java half has to go through `aapt2` and `d8` to become an APK. A sysl package has no
// way to carry that and no business trying to: `13 §1` describes a module of sysl source, and an
// Android activity is neither.

package {
  name    = "syslui-android"
  version = "0.1.0"

  // Imports became transitive in 0.0.73, which is what lets the block below name one coordinate.
  sysl = "0.0.73"
}

// **One dependency, and the same one a desktop program would name.** `15 §8` says a link directive
// names a library and never a path, so nothing about `sh.sysl.sdl3` changes between a Mac and a
// phone: identical headers, identical `@link("SDL3")`, identical binding. What changes is *where the
// library is*, which is a fact about the machine being built on — and `CMakeLists.txt` is where this
// project answers it, out of the AAR that `fetch-sdl3.sh` downloads.
//
// That is why there is no `sdl3-android` package and never will be. The org's rule against carrying
// a prebuilt `.so` in a package is the other half of it: SDL's AAR is 16 MB of binaries built
// against an NDK and an API level somebody else chose, and a package hashing that into `sysl.sum`
// would be shipping a build nobody in this org could reproduce.
// **One coordinate and no `--lib`.** Imports are transitive as of sysl 0.0.73, so naming the driver
// reaches syslUI through it — and this program never names SDL3 or PlutoVG at all, because the
// driver is what touches them.
dependencies {
  syslui-sdl { git = "github.com/sysl-lang/syslui-sdl", version = "0.1.0" }
}

// `heap` and `posix` are what `sh.sysl.sdl3` itself requires, and a program reaching a gated module
// has to say so too. Android answers both: Bionic is POSIX — `Os.Android` is in the compiler's POSIX
// set, and the standard library's `__posix__` directories cover it with nothing written for Android
// specifically.
requires {
  heap  = true
  posix = true
}
